# Production and Costs: From Inputs to Supply

> Firms convert inputs into output under the discipline of cost; the
> cost curve is the seed from which every supply decision grows.

[definition]
**Production** is the transformation of inputs (factors) into output.
The **production function** records the maximum output obtainable from
each combination of inputs for a given technology.
[/definition]

## 1. Factors of Production and Their Rewards

| Factor | Meaning | Factor reward |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Land | All natural resources — soil, minerals, water | Rent |
| Labour | Physical and mental human effort | Wages |
| Capital | Man-made aids: machines, buildings, tools | Interest |
| Entrepreneurship | Organises factors, bears risk, innovates | Profit |

## 2. Total, Average, and Marginal Product

With one variable input (labour) added to fixed land and capital:

- **Total product (TP)** — output from all units of input.
- **Average product** — output per unit of input: `AP = TP / L`.
- **Marginal product** — extra output from one more unit:
  `MP = ΔTP / ΔL`.

| Workers | TP (baskets) | AP | MP |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 10 | 10 | 10 |
| 2 | 24 | 12 | 14 |
| 3 | 39 | 13 | 15 |
| 4 | 52 | 13 | 13 |
| 5 | 60 | 12 | 8 |
| 6 | 63 | 10.5 | 3 |
| 7 | 63 | 9 | 0 |
| 8 | 60 | 7.5 | −3 |

## 3. The Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns

[important]
As more and more units of a variable input are added to a **fixed**
input, the marginal product eventually falls. It is a short-run law
about *changing proportions* of inputs, not about technology failing.
[/important]

Three stages emerge — rational production stops before the third:

1. **Increasing returns** — MP rises (workers 1–3).
2. **Diminishing returns** — MP falls but positive (workers 4–7).
3. **Negative returns** — MP below zero; TP itself falls (worker 8).

## 4. Short Run vs Long Run

The **short run** is the period in which at least one factor is fixed
(usually capital). The **long run** is long enough for *all* inputs to
vary — new plants can be built, old ones closed. In the long run there
are no fixed costs, only variable ones.

## 5. Cost Concepts and Curves

- **Fixed costs (TFC)** — do not vary with output: rent, salaries of
  permanent staff, insurance. TFC is a horizontal line.
- **Variable costs (TVC)** — rise with output: raw material, power,
  piece-rate wages.
- **Total cost**: `TC = TFC + TVC`.

Per-unit measures: `AC = TC / Q` and `MC = ΔTC / ΔQ`.

| Output | TFC (₹) | TVC (₹) | TC (₹) | AC (₹) | MC (₹) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 60 | 0 | 60 | — | — |
| 1 | 60 | 30 | 90 | 90 | 30 |
| 2 | 60 | 50 | 110 | 55 | 20 |
| 3 | 60 | 66 | 126 | 42 | 16 |
| 4 | 60 | 84 | 144 | 36 | 18 |
| 5 | 60 | 110 | 170 | 34 | 26 |
| 6 | 60 | 150 | 210 | 35 | 40 |

[info title="The AC–MC relationship"]
While MC is below AC it drags AC down; once MC crosses above AC it
pulls AC up — so the MC curve cuts the AC curve exactly at AC's
**minimum point** (output 5 above). The same logic averages any
quantity: a new member changes the team average toward their own value.
[/info]

## 6. Explicit vs Implicit Costs

| Type | Definition | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Explicit | Actual money paid to outsiders | Wages, rent, power bills |
| Implicit | Opportunity cost of self-owned resources | Owner's forgone salary, own building not rented out |

**Economic cost** = explicit + implicit. **Accounting profit** counts
only explicit costs, so it can be positive while economic profit is
negative — the moment a shopkeeper should honestly compare earnings
with the best alternative job.

## 7. Economies and Diseconomies of Scale

In the long run, expanding *scale* changes average cost:

- **Economies of scale** — AC falls with size: technical (larger,
  more efficient machines), managerial (specialist departments),
  marketing (bulk advertising), financial (cheaper credit), risk
  spreading (multiple products).
- **Diseconomies** — beyond some size, coordination and motivation
  problems raise AC: layers of management, diluted accountability.
- **Minimum efficient scale** — the smallest output at which AC hits
  its minimum; it determines how many firms an industry can support.

@diagram tree id="scale-economies" layout="tree" aria="Sources of economies of scale"
  "Economies of Scale" -> Technical
  "Economies of Scale" -> Managerial
  "Economies of Scale" -> Marketing
  "Economies of Scale" -> Financial
  "Economies of Scale" -> "Risk Spreading"
@/diagram

## 8. Quick Revision Table

| Concept | One-line rule |
| --- | --- |
| Law of variable proportions | MP of a variable input eventually falls against fixed input |
| Stage of rational production | Stage two — MP falling but positive |
| TC | TFC + TVC, starts from TFC at zero output |
| MC vs AC | MC cuts AC at its minimum |
| Short run | Some factor fixed, fixed costs exist |
| Long run | All factors variable, only decisions about scale |
| Economies of scale | AC falls as plant size grows (long run) |
| Economic cost | Explicit + implicit (opportunity) cost |

[summary]
Diminishing marginal returns explains the short-run cost curve's rise;
economies and diseconomies of scale shape the long-run curve. Where MC
meets price, the firm finds its supply — production theory is simply
supply's engine room.
[/summary]
